+### [Instant Neural Graphics Primitives (Instant NGP)](8637-instant-neural-graphics-primitives-instant-ngp.md)
+Replaces the MLP with a Multi-resolution Hash Encoding. It differs from standard NeRF by drastically reducing training time and enabling real-time rendering.
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Training is slow, typically requiring 10–20 hours on a single NVIDIA A100 GPU for synthetic scenes [S1][S6, unofficial]. Rendering a single novel view takes approximately 30 seconds [S1].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** The MLP weights are small (~5 MB) [S4, unofficial], but rendering requires storing intermediate transmittance and alpha values for all samples along all rays, leading to high GPU memory usage during training (mitigated by chunking rays into batches of 4096) [S6, unofficial][S12, unofficial].
-*   **Performance:** On the NeRF Synthetic (Blender) dataset, NeRF achieves a PSNR of ~31.0 dB [S1]. It is slower than [Instant-NGP](8539-graf.md) (which trains in minutes) and [3D Gaussian Splatting](6569-point-nerf.md) (real-time), but provides higher quality than early hash-grid methods [S1].
-*   **Strengths:** High-quality novel view synthesis with accurate view-dependent effects (specularities, transparency) and robust handling of complex geometry without explicit mesh extraction [S1][S2, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations:** Slow training and rendering speeds; prone to aliasing artifacts at varying resolutions (addressed by [Mip-NeRF](3381-mip-nerf.md)); density field does not explicitly represent surfaces, making mesh extraction difficult [S1].
+
+*   **Time Complexity:** Training is slow, typically taking 10-20 hours on a single GPU for a single scene [S1]. Rendering is also slow, taking ~30 seconds per image [S1].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** The MLP weights are small (~5MB), but the training process requires storing intermediate activations for backpropagation, leading to high memory usage during optimization [S5].
+*   **Performance:** On the NeRF Synthetic (Blender) dataset, NeRF achieves a PSNR of ~31.0 dB [S1]. It provides high-quality novel view synthesis but suffers from long training times and slow rendering speeds compared to later methods like Instant-NGP or 3D Gaussian Splatting [S1].
+*   **Strengths:** High photorealism, handles complex view-dependent effects (specularity, transparency), and produces smooth continuous representations.
+*   **Limitations:** Slow training and rendering, poor surface reconstruction accuracy (density is not a true SDF), and requires careful tuning of sampling and encoding parameters [S1].
 
 ## Background
-NeRF was introduced by Mildenhall et al. in 2020 [S1][S3, unofficial]. It established the field of neural radiance fields by demonstrating that a simple MLP, optimized via differentiable volume rendering, could synthesize photorealistic novel views from sparse input images. The method builds on earlier neural implicit representations [S5] and positional encoding techniques [S9, unofficial]. It is widely used in virtual reality, film production, and 3D asset creation [S1].
+
+NeRF was introduced by Mildenhall et al. in 2020 [S1][S3, unofficial]. It established the field of neural radiance fields by representing a scene as a continuous 5D function (3D position + 2D viewing direction) mapping to color and density. The method was trained to reproduce input views and rendered novel views through differentiable ray marching. Prior to NeRF, implicit representations struggled with view-dependent effects and high-frequency details. NeRF's key innovations were the asymmetric MLP architecture for view-dependent color and the use of positional encoding combined with hierarchical sampling [S1][S3, unofficial].
 
